Apple has reportedly revised its plans for adopting OLED displays across its MacBook lineup, and its existing LCD-based MacBook Pro models with mini-LED backlighting are now expected to remain available for longer than anticipated.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/04/MacBook-Pro-Low-Angle-Wide-Lens-2.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1046018" /><br/>
According to a new OLED adoption roadmap published by research firm <a href="https://omdiaevents.com/china-display-conference/comparison-and-analysis-of-oled-processes/">Omdia</a>, Apple still intends long-term adoption of OLED panels across MacBooks and iPads, but the transition pace has recently changed, owing to higher memory and component costs.<br/>
<br/>
Previously, Omdia had expected Apple to discontinue its existing LCD-based models sooner, once its 14-inch and 16-inch OLED MacBook Pro models arrive – either this year or in early 2027. <br/>
<br/>
However, Omdia now predicts that Apple will continue to sell both 14-inch and 16-inch LCD MacBook Pro models in parallel with the OLED MacBook Pro product line into at least 2028, a product strategy arrangement that is said to be based on cost factors.<br/>
<br/>
